Complete Skeleton, S: Given ad-dimensional space, the intersection of dhyperplanes is a
0-dimensional point which is called the vertex. The intersection of d−1hyperplanes creates a
1-dimensional line segment which is called an edge. The endpoints of an edge consist of two vertices
which are called neighbors. These vertices and edges create a connected graph called Complete
Skeleton (S). The details about the complete skeleton can be found in [11].
k-set: Given a set of npoints ind-dimensional space, a k-set is a subset of kpoints that can be
separated by a hyperplane from the remaining points. Much is known about k-sets, their counts, as
well as algorithms for their enumeration and construction, especially in lower dimensional space
[11]. For our geometric mapping, consider the npoints is given by the data points in the primal
space. As the optimal quantile regression hyperplane H∗must partition the npoints such that τn
points are on one side and the remaining (1−τ)nlie on the other, the optimal hyperplane is a
separating k-set hyperplane. We exploit these observations to design our deterministic strongly
polynomial algorithm for 2(and higher) dimensions.
Arrangements : Givennhyperplanes in d-dimensional space, the entire space is partitioned into
anarrangement consisting of O(nd)d-dimensional convex cells. Much is known about the geometric
properties of such arrangements, as well as algorithms for their construction [ 11]. Thenpoints in
the primal space transform into ndual hyperplanes, creating a dissection of the dual space into an
arrangement [12]. Figure 4 shows the lines in the dual space for the sample points in Figure 3.
k-level of an arrangement: In an arrangement of nhyperplane, the k-level of an arrangement is
a set of points psuch that the number of lines above p≤k−1and and the number of lines below
p≤n−k. The black bold lines in Figure 4 shows the 4-level of arrangement.
Further details of duality, arrangements, complete skeleton graph, k-sets andk-level of arrange-
ment can be found in [11].
3 Efficiently Updating Neighbors, and the Use of k-Sets
As seen in Section 2, a vertex in the complete skeleton graph of the arrangement in dual space
refers to a QR hyperplane in primal space. The optimal solution corresponds to one of these vertices
in the complete skeleton graph. Note that each of these vertices (QR hyperplane in primal space)
has an optimization function value associated with it. Computing the optimization from scratch
at any vertex takes O(nd)time, and there are O(nd)such vertices. In this section we propose
two improvements to this basic brute force approach: (a) design an efficient way to incrementally
calculate the optimization function at a vertex from precomputed values at a neighboring vertex
(along the complete skeleton graph), and (b) trim the complete skeleton graph to only consider the
vertices corresponding to k-sets.
3.1 UpdateNeighbor subroutine
In this subsection, we propose an UpdateNeighbor subroutine which can efficiently and incre-
mentally calculate the objective function at a vertex of the complete skeleton graph, by leveraging
the calculations performed at a neighboring vertex. The incremental computation only takes O(d)
time at a vertex, as compared to O(nd)time if performed from scratch. The update operation relies
on maintaining a few aggregate values when moving from one vertex to its neighboring vertex. To
