3.2 Improving the Naive Baseline Using UpdateNeighbor subroutine
An algorithm that solely relies on UpdateNeighbor subroutine to find the optimal QRline can use
a neighborhood exploration in the complete skeleton graph S. Initially, a vertex is Sis arbitrarily
chosen as a start vertex. In each step of the algorithm, the neighborhood is explored. The vertex
which improves the optimization function the most is visited next. The exploration continues until
there are no neighboring vertices with a lower value for the optimization function. In the worst
case, this algorithm may explore the complete skeleton graph, and its worst case time complexity is
thereforeO(dnd). Nevertheless, this algorithm is strongly polynomial under the assumption that d
is bounded, and is an improvement over the naive baseline algorithm. A more detailed explanation
for this algorithm is presented in Appendix C.1.
3.3 QReg2D : Leveraging k-Sets in the Two Dimensional QR Problem
Recall from Section 2.3 that a k-set is a subset containing kpoints that can be separated from
the rest of the (n−k)points by a hyperplane. An interesting observation is that the optimal QR
hyperplane separates the npoints intoτnand(1−τ)npoints [24]. While there are many hyperplanes
that separate npoints into two parts (with τnand(1−τ)npoints), we are interested in the specific
hyperplane that provides the lowest optimization score. Since a k-set separate the points into kand
n−kpoints,the optimal QR hyperplane must correspond to a k-set, wherek=τn. In
other words, the optimal QR hyperplane is among the set of possible k-set separating hyperplanes,
the one with minimum optimization score.
